---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import GuideLayout from "../../components/GuideLayout.astro";
import { watershedCodeTheme } from "../../styles/code-theme";

const beam = `import gleam/erlang/process
import gleam/json
import watershed/channel.{type ChannelEvent}
import watershed_beam

type Msg {
  MapChanged(ChannelEvent)
}

pub fn main() {
  // Blocks until the op history has replayed locally.
  let assert Ok(doc) =
    watershed_beam.connect(
      host: "127.0.0.1",
      port: 4000,
      tenant: "flow-co",
      document: "flowboard",
      token: token,
      user_id: "ada",
    )
  let board = watershed_beam.root(doc)
  let events = watershed_beam.subscribe(board)
  let selector =
    process.new_selector()
    |> process.select_map(events, MapChanged)

  // Subscribe first: this local write emits immediately.
  watershed_beam.set(board, "title", json.string("Q3 sprint board"))

  // The same subject also receives remote changes as they are applied.
  let MapChanged(_event) = process.selector_receive_forever(selector)
}`;

const js = `import gleam/io
import gleam/json
import watershed.{WatershedConfig}

pub fn main() {
  let config =
    WatershedConfig(
      url: "ws://127.0.0.1:4000/socket/websocket?vsn=2.0.0",
      tenant: "flow-co",
      document: "flowboard",
      token: token,
      user_id: "ada",
    )

  // Returns immediately; on_ready fires after handshake + history replay.
  let doc =
    watershed.connect(config, on_ready: fn(result) {
      case result {
        Ok(Nil) -> io.println("synced")
        Error(reason) -> io.println("rejected: " <> reason)
      }
    })

  let board = watershed.root(doc)
}`;
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="connect"
  description="Step one of the watershed build guide: connect to a named, server-sequenced document, read its root map, and understand the optimistic-then-sequenced edit model, on both the BEAM and browser targets."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>A document is a named container of channels</h2>
    <p>
      watershed synchronizes data in a <strong>document</strong>. The server
      identifies this container by a <code>tenant</code> and a
      <code>document</code> ID. Clients that connect to the same pair share the
      same state. Flowboard’s teammates all open
      <code>flow-co / flowboard</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      During connection, watershed replays the document’s operation history to
      reproduce current state. It then keeps the connection open and receives
      all later changes in server order.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Connect</h2>
    <p>
      watershed uses the same pure core on the BEAM and in the browser. Both
      facades provide the same structure operations. Their connection and
      subscription APIs differ because the target runtimes differ. Select your
      target:
    </p>

    <div class="g-tabs">
      <input type="radio" name="target" id="t-beam" checked />
      <input type="radio" name="target" id="t-js" />
      <div class="g-tab-row" role="tablist" aria-label="Target">
        <label for="t-beam">BEAM · <code>watershed_beam</code></label>
        <label for="t-js">Browser · <code>watershed</code></label>
      </div>
      <div class="g-tab-panels">
        <figure class="g-code g-panel g-panel-beam">
          <Code code={beam} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
          <figcaption class="annot">
            src/flowboard.gleam · erlang target
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="g-code g-panel g-panel-js">
          <Code code={js} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
          <figcaption class="annot">
            src/flowboard.gleam · javascript target
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>Connection behavior differs by target.</strong> On the BEAM,
        <code>connect</code> blocks and returns <code>Result(Document, _)</code>
        after history replay. In the browser, it returns at once and calls
        <code>on_ready</code> after the handshake because the event loop cannot
        block. Keep the document handle for diagnostics and document services.
        Attach or ensure channels only after
        <code>on_ready</code> reports <code>Ok</code>. Treating handle-arrival
        and readiness as one event causes a first-load race. Map subscriptions
        also differ. BEAM returns an OTP
        <code>Subject(MapEvent)</code>; the browser facade registers a callback
        directly. Step 05 converts browser callbacks to Lustre effects.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Read the root, write optimistically</h2>
    <p>
      Each document has a root <code>SharedMap</code> at address
      <code>"root"</code>. Get it with <code>root(doc)</code>. Use
      <code>set</code>, <code>get</code>, and <code>subscribe</code> as you
      would for an ordinary map. You do not write a merge callback.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <code>set</code> applies to your local replica <em>immediately</em> and
      emits a local subscription event. Subscribe before you make edits.
      Remote writes emit when the runtime applies them to your replica. Both
      event types must read visible state through the same render path. A server
      acknowledgment can emit no event when confirmation does not change
      that visible state.
    </p>
    <p>
      The server still assigns the order that resolves concurrent writes.
      Subscriptions tell your app when its local view changed; sequencing tells
      replicas which value wins. For a map, the last sequenced write wins per
      key.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can store a string under <code>"title"</code>, but repeated key
      literals increase the risk of errors. The next step declares keys and
      their types in one place.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">You can now</span>
    <p>
      Open a live document from either target. Read and write its root map.
      Local edits appear at once and converge in server order.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  /* target toggle — radios styled as printed cells, like the demo picker.
     Pure CSS: the checked radio reveals its panel and inks its label. */
  .g-tabs {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .g-tabs input {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .g-tab-row {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--ink);
    width: fit-content;
    max-width: 100%;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  .g-tab-row label {
    padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-annot);
    color: var(--muted);
    cursor: pointer;
    user-select: none;
    transition:
      background var(--dur-feedback) var(--ease-out-quart),
      color var(--dur-feedback) var(--ease-out-quart);
  }

  .g-tab-row label:first-of-type {
    border-right: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }

  .g-tab-row label code {
    font-size: 1em;
    color: inherit;
  }

  #t-beam:checked ~ .g-tab-row label[for="t-beam"],
  #t-js:checked ~ .g-tab-row label[for="t-js"] {
    background: var(--ink);
    color: var(--bg);
  }

  #t-beam:focus-visible ~ .g-tab-row label[for="t-beam"],
  #t-js:focus-visible ~ .g-tab-row label[for="t-js"] {
    outline: 2px solid var(--overprint);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .g-panel {
    display: none;
  }

  #t-beam:checked ~ .g-tab-panels .g-panel-beam,
  #t-js:checked ~ .g-tab-panels .g-panel-js {
    display: block;
  }
</style>
